concepts.content:
A radiance field models a scene by assigning a color and volumetric density to each point in 3D space. It represents a portion of the plenoptic function based on the idea that the optical content of a scene can be modeled as a cloud of colorful particles with varying levels of transparency. This representation can be rendered into images using volume rendering.

A radiance field $L$ is defined as a mapping from scene coordinates to color and density (transparency) values. The coordinates can be 2D ($X,Y$) or 3D ($X,Y,Z$) and may include angular dimensions ($\psi$ and $\phi$) to represent the viewing angle. Representing color with $r,g,b$ and density with a scalar $\sigma$, the function is expressed as:

$$\begin{aligned}
    L: X,Y,Z,\psi,\phi \rightarrow r,g,b,\sigma \quad\quad\triangleleft \quad\text{radiance field}
\end{aligned}$$

The angular coordinates allow an object's color to vary depending on the viewing angle, which is necessary for modeling shiny surfaces. The subcomponents $L^c$ and $L^\sigma$ denote the outputs for color and density respectively:

$$\begin{aligned}
    L^c&: X,Y,Z,\psi,\phi \rightarrow r,g,b\\
    L^{\sigma}&: X,Y,Z,\psi,\phi \rightarrow\sigma
\end{aligned}$$

